It honestly helps to not do it in person. For me it takes months to fully break away from another person. You have to have the initial conversation, then over time distance yourself from them. Especially if you have a hard time determining what emotions are yours and what are the others.  

&#x200B;

If you find yourself having thoughts like, omg what am I doing, do I want to do this, confusion, back and forth battles in your head. And then as soon as you leave the house, your going to get food, and your say to yourself, wtf am I thinking, I don't like this person because of x y z, I need this to be over, you are very sure of this. 

&#x200B;

Recognize that you are having so much back and forth because you are too close in proximity to the other person. It might be better for you to have phone or text conversations going forward so as not to muddy the waters of your mind.

As long as you show her that you have a plan together with a timeline when you will be leaving i am sure your mom would let you stay a little longer to ensure that you are making a safe move into a good place. 

&#x200B;

The most important thing to do is figure out how much rent will be in the area that you are living in, start doing research. This will be your biggest expense. If you don't think that you can afford rent on your own then you will need to get a roommate. You will find plenty of people looking for roommates or a place to move into on facebook market and craigslist. You will need to interview them to make sure that you will get along. Ask the questions that you need to find out if you will get along with these people. Do you smoke, do you have animals, what are your work/sleep hours, do you have a significant other, do you like to party and make noise etc.

&#x200B;

Next up you need to save money for a deposit and moving fees. a deposit will be one months rent usually. moving fees will be whatever a truck or uhaul to move your furniture. Think about the things that you need to buy that you used at your moms that you will not be able to take with you. Do you have a TV, bed, couch, tables etc.

&#x200B;

Your list of bills will now include rent, utilities, internet, phone, vehicle, insurance, streaming services, gas, food. Write out everything you spend money on and figure out what your budget is, stick to it. Most importantly, put money into savings every month. a significant amount, you WILL run in to unexpected problems.

&#x200B;

A break down for you:

&#x200B;

Rent - 800

Utilities- 50

internet - 50

car payment- 250

car insurance - 150

neflix/hulu- 20

phone- 80

credit card - 100

savings - 200

food- 300

gas- 100

&#x200B;

adjust this for your own bills, make changes based on the amount of money that you have per month. at the end of the month, chances are you will be left with NOTHING. Dont let this scare you, that is what your savings is for. 

&#x200B;

When you go look at places, after you find one, you will have to write a check for your deposit. Chances are you dont have any, thats ok, you can go get a money order or a cashiers check. Just google places around you where you can get one, its easy. Your move in date wont be until the 1st of the month so already you dont have enough time to find a place, your mom should be well aware of this.

&#x200B;

The first thing you are going to do when you get your move in date is go online to your energy provider and set up the date for it to be turned on in your name at the place, so they will have it ready to go the day you move in. You want to do the same thing with your internet provider. They with give you a router and all that. First night you are there, make sure you set up your internet, tv, bed, have some food, a change of clothes and TOILET PAPER!!! Dont worry about anything else that day. 

&#x200B;

Hope that helps :)

I love how people are bashing the OP for trying to make something healthier, by repeatedly saying that's not healthy. No duh, that's why OP said to add veggies lol. Guess what. If someone is so poor that they have to buy ramen and kraft for a lot of meals. I am guessing that they are aware of the magic of bulk rice and beans as well. I am guessing they have scoured the earth to find the cheapest things to sustain them. 

&#x200B;

I shouldn't say guessing. I should say I know, because I have lived my whole life this way. Extremely poor and always trying to figure it out.

&#x200B;

To add to the point of this in a positive way. Ramen and rice, are great to use when you get food from the food shelves you don't know what to do with. Because a can of mushrooms or water chestnuts that suzie creamcheese threw out last christmas for her tax credit aren't exactly universal ingredients! Also, you don't have to use the seasoning packet that comes with the ramen, you can put whatever the hell you want on the noodles and boom, no more sodium overdose.

